title: Syncing two repositories
tags: Sync
kind: guide
summary: Load both repositories, write an intents file naming what to keep in step, plan, review, run — then plan again whenever you want them in step again.

!! Once

Both repositories are loaded in the same daemon (`mf repo load`), and their schemas
are identical ([[Why schemas must be identical]]). An ''intents file'' says what
to keep in step — one query per repository, run on that repository
([[The intents file]]):

```
[[intents]]
repo  = "laptop"
query = 'mfr_path ->* "/projects"'

[[intents]]
repo  = "nas"
query = 'tag = "family"'
```

!! Each time

```
mf sync plan laptop nas --intents sync.toml
mf sync show laptop nas
mf sync run laptop nas
```

<<cmd "mf sync plan">> matches what the queries select to records of the other side,
compares every linked pair, and writes the result into the plan: it prints the plan
repository's uuid and the number of operations. A field changed on both sides is a
''conflict'': the plan asks which side wins, unless a rule of the intents file or
`--on-conflict` answers ([[Sync conflicts]]). Nothing in the two repositories is
changed yet. The order of the two repositories on the command line does not
matter.

<<cmd "mf sync show">> reviews the plan: how many operations of each kind, and,
in red, those the run would skip because a record changed since the plan
(`--conflicts`, `--files` and `--summary` narrow it). The plan is a repository like
any other ([[The sync plan]]): a conflict's resolution can still be changed there.

<<cmd "mf sync run">> asks for a confirmation (`--yes` skips it), carries the plan
out, and reports what it did and what it skipped ([[Running a sync]]). A skipped
operation stays in the plan; the next `mf sync plan` sees whatever it left
undone.

<<cmd "mf sync status">> tells, per link, whether the two records are still as the
last sync left them. The next sync only works on what changed since.

!! In the GUI

The [[sync panel]] does the same four steps, without a shell.
